# CODEOWNERS — meta-integrity of the guard/validation system.
# Contract: specs/spec-dev-validation.md § Guard-System Meta-Integrity
# (req-dev-validation-meta-integrity-2, the out-of-band anchor).
#
# The enforcement MACHINERY below is owner-review-always: with "Require review from
# Code Owners" enabled in branch protection, any PR touching one of these paths needs
# @notgeorge's explicit approval. The point is that disabling a gate — neutering a
# check(), dropping a guard, or editing the runner/CI so the suite stops running —
# becomes a deliberate, reviewed, human act, even from an automated or AI actor.
#
# IMPORTANT — this file is a no-op without the platform settings it presumes:
#   1. Branch protection on `main`: PRs only, "Require review from Code Owners" ON,
#      the guard/gate status check (CodeBuild `test_all` lane) required + non-bypassable.
#   2. GitHub SILENTLY IGNORES an owner it cannot resolve (wrong handle / no write
#      access). This repo lives in the unified-systems-com org (transferred from
#      notgeorge 2026-08-08); @notgeorge is George's USERNAME and still resolves because
#      he is the org owner — the handle names a person, not the repo's home. If his
#      access ever changes (or ownership moves to a team, e.g. @unified-systems-com/...),
#      update this file or the protection quietly becomes a no-op.
#   3. TWO-ACCOUNT REVIEW (2026-08-21): @criticalsec is George's second account,
#      co-owner on every owned path so that owned-path PRs authored by @notgeorge (who
#      cannot approve his own PRs) block until the second account approves. The second
#      account NEVER authenticates on the dev laptop — approvals come from a separate
#      device, or this control is theater against a compromised session. If
#      @criticalsec loses write access it silently stops resolving (see 2).
# This file owns itself (via /.github/ below), so weakening it is itself a gated change.
#
# Policy DATA is deliberately NOT owned here (req-dev-validation-meta-integrity-1): a
# ratchet baseline *shrinking* and a *coverage-adding* change (a new guard/surface) are
# the self-safe directions. Only weakening moves want review.

# ---- enforcement machinery: owner review always ----
/tap/guards/                             @notgeorge @criticalsec
/tap/source_scan.py                      @notgeorge @criticalsec
/tap/direct_write_coverage.py            @notgeorge @criticalsec
/tap/authz_coverage.py                   @notgeorge @criticalsec
/tap/ratchet.py                          @notgeorge @criticalsec
/tap/tests/test_guards.py                @notgeorge @criticalsec
/tap_boot/management/commands/guards.py  @notgeorge @criticalsec
/pyproject.toml                          @notgeorge @criticalsec
/.github/                                @notgeorge @criticalsec
/ci/terraform/                           @notgeorge @criticalsec
/scripts/gate                            @notgeorge @criticalsec
/scripts/gate-lean                       @notgeorge @criticalsec
/scripts/promote-to-main.sh              @notgeorge @criticalsec
/scripts/promote-all-sessions.sh         @notgeorge @criticalsec

# every guards/ package — core, per-app, and per-plugin
**/guards/                               @notgeorge @criticalsec

# ---- build/exec plumbing (req-cicd-ai-review-least-privilege-5): the surfaces where a
#      single change defeats every other control. Inbound "improvements" here get the
#      heaviest scrutiny; reviewer prompts flag these diffs as findings in their own right. ----
/Dockerfile*                             @notgeorge @criticalsec
/docker/                                 @notgeorge @criticalsec
/docker-compose*.yml                     @notgeorge @criticalsec
/.githooks/                              @notgeorge @criticalsec

# ---- canon that redirects work: the philosophy corpus and the demand/intent layer.
#      WOG entries are cited by name from doctrine (WOG-Oneness, WOG-Chaotic-Majority), so
#      an edit to a settled entry silently changes what canon means everywhere it is cited,
#      and a promote/demote between tier files changes an entry's authority. The roadmap and
#      product map are read as on-path authority by every human and AI thread (CLAUDE.md /
#      AGENTS.md point at them), so an unreviewed edit to a step's Objective, Done-Test or
#      Non-Goals silently redirects what gets built. Dates are deliberately not here — they
#      live in the milestone a step's `Tracking:` line points at (req-roadmap-dates-in-tracker)
#      — so the fence is what this ownership protects. Governance acts, not edits. ----
/wog/                                    @notgeorge @criticalsec
/plan/                                   @notgeorge @criticalsec

# ---- conduct + governance are NOT owned here: CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md and GOVERNANCE.md
#      moved to unified-systems-com/.github as org-wide default community health files,
#      so every repo inherits them. Their ownership (incl. @flowingcalm as conduct/
#      governance steward) lives in that repo's CODEOWNERS. Do not re-add a copy here —
#      a repo's own file OVERRIDES the org default and would silently fork policy. ----

# ---- policy data carved back out (NO owner): ratchet baselines shrink freely; the
#      ratchet's stale-entry tooth already forbids fabricated growth. Must stay last so
#      last-match-wins removes ownership for these paths. ----
